1. when you type " git init " -> " .git " hidden file is created in your folder and maintains records locally
2. Some commands of terminal -  
                    (1) mkdir -> "make directary" means -> a new folder
                    (2) ls  -> "list"-> list all files and folder in present directorary.
                    (3) cd -> "change directoray" -> gets inside a folder
                    (4) ls -a. -> hidden files
                    (5) touch -> to create a file
                    (6) rm-rf <file_name> -> to delete the files.
3. when you type " git add ."-> add all changes. "git add <file_name>" -> a single file change is added.
4. when you type " git commit -m "message" " -> commit changes and m means message.
5. git status -> status of git.
6. git restore --staged <file_name> -> to uncommit the file.
7. git log -> to watch entire modifies history.
8. git reset "commitID" -> it is like when you want to go to a particular stage of code from previous codebase.
9. git stash -> it like you have done some changes but you don't want to save them but you also don't want to delete them you want those chages after some time then this command is used.
10. git stash pop -> to call the code from unstaged area.
11. git stash clear -> to delete those unstaged changes.
12. git remote add origin <github repo link> -> to link the githun repo to git.
13. git remote -v -> to check which repo is connected to the folder. 
14. git push origin main -> to push them to github.
15. git branch "branch_name"-> create a branch , always commit to branch.
16. git checkout branchname -> pointer shift to brach.
17. git rebase i "commit id" -> merge above commits in a single commit by squashing and picking.

How to do changes in some opensource project in gothub or contribute.
1. first open them in your on own account by clicking on project.
2. then, clone it in you local computer
by git clone <github link> -> this will download all files of that project and you can work on that.
3. from where you taken the url for project is call -> upstream url.
4. git remote upstream 'url"
5. create a branch and edit the project.
6. git push origin "branch name"
7. git pull upstream main -> to fetch commits from main projects.
8. git push origin main -> to match commits with main projects.